export const RILL_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"RILL\" (image-led watershed landing page)\n\nBuild a landing page in **Next.js (App Router) + React + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS v4** for a fictional field-hydrology studio called **RILL**. RILL helps dryland farms hold water in the soil through contour planning, low-pressure irrigation, and living-soil practices.\n\nThe central metaphor is **a farm is a watershed, so the page follows one drop from ridge to root zone**. Use generated documentary stills and short clips as the main visual attention. On desktop, the `Read the land` chapter is a `360svh` section with a `100svh` sticky stage. Scroll moves through ridge, swale, storage and root-zone media plates. A real 0–80 mm range input changes the shared rain value, the water-budget readout, the chapter rail and the animated contour drop. At 42 mm the runoff line breaks over the ridge footage, crosses the contour and settles into the root zone.\n\n## Brand\n\n- Slogan: **Make every drop take the long way.**\n- Positioning: **Field hydrology for farms that keep water in the ground.**\n- Voice: measured, observant, grounded, specific, hopeful. Avoid greenwashed, extractive, hyped, frictionless or disposable language.\n- Palette: basalt `#14231E`, limestone `#F0E9DB`, iron oxide `#B85C43`, riverstone `#5B8174`, lichen `#87956C`, silt `#C9B79E`. Use `color-mix()` for tints; do not add hex colours.\n- Type through `next/font/google`: Cormorant Garamond 500/600 normal and italic for display, Fira Sans 400/500/600 for body, Fira Mono 400/500 for coordinates and readouts.\n- Logo: three contour lines make a rivulet and a small oxide drop; provide `brand/Logo.tsx`, `icon.svg`, and a 1200×630 `opengraph-image.tsx`.\n- Corners: 6px media frames, 8px cards and full pill buttons. Vary alignment across sections: centred hero/budget moments, left field records, right-anchored ecosystem detail.\n\n## Sections\n\n1. Fixed header with RILL mark, chapter links, live rainfall and a sliding active indicator.\n2. Hero storm film with a full-bleed generated ridge image/clip, headline “Read the land. Keep the rain.” and `Book a field walk` CTA.\n3. Read the land sticky watershed stage with generated ridge, swale, storage and root-zone plates, contour SVG, drop marker and range input.\n4. Water budget with live runoff, storage and root-zone values. Status is `dry` below 18 mm, `holding` from 18–41 mm, and `ready` at 42 mm or above.\n5. Field systems cards for contour swale, shade row and low-pressure drip; cards lift, tilt and spotlight on fine pointers.\n6. Field notes rail with three specific records: Cienega Fields 14 acres / 42 mm / 31 days, Marrow Creek 28 acres / 36 mm / 24 days, Las Lomas 42 acres / 51 mm / 38 days.\n7. Seasonal ledger with twelve month buttons and directional detail changes.\n8. Field-walk form with name, farm, email, acreage and goal. Validate locally, show field errors, morph through loading, then replace the form with a success panel. Never send a network request.\n9. Ecosystem footer diagram with soil, roots, mycelium, crop and a water line, field coordinates and the line “Keep water in the ground.”\n\n## Pure logic\n\nCreate and test `motion/math.ts`, `watershed/water.ts`, `watershed/store.ts`, and `forms/validation.ts` before their UI components.\n\n- `progressFromOffsets(rectTop, rectHeight, viewportHeight)` clamps a sticky section to 0–1.\n- `rainfallAt(progress)` maps 0–1 to 0–80 mm; `progressAtRainfall(mm)` is its inverse.\n- `waterBudget(mm)` uses `runoff = clamp(0, 100, max(0, mm - 26) * 1.2)`, `storage = clamp(0, 100, mm * .92 - runoff * .24)`, `rootZone = clamp(0, 100, storage * .74)`.\n- `watershedPhase(progress)` returns ridge / swale / storage / root-zone at 0.2, 0.5 and 0.78.\n- `contourPoint(progress)` returns a deterministic SVG point.\n- The external store exposes `getSnapshot`, `subscribe` and `setRain` and de-duplicates unchanged values.\n\n## Motion and accessibility\n\nMotion identity is organic / analog with the verbs **trace** and **settle**. Use easing `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)` for arrivals, `cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)` for handoffs, and the spring `linear(0, 0.16 12%, 0.76 28%, 1.06 50%, 0.98 74%, 1)`. Durations are 90ms, 180ms, 480ms, 920ms and 1200ms; stagger is 80ms. Use mask-rise text, clip-path media wipes, drawn rules, staggered cards, count-up values and a single bounded scroll progress loop. Animate only transform, opacity, clip-path and SVG stroke offsets.\n\nProvide one `h1`, logical headings, landmarks, named controls, visible focus rings, `aria-live` only for phase changes, and final values that remain in text. Under reduced motion, disable pinning, smooth scrolling, video playback and ambient loops while keeping every image, value and form state visible. With JavaScript disabled, show the same story in normal flow. On touch, do not rely on hover.\n\n## Media\n\nUse the platform-neutral prompts in `docs/image-prompts/rill.md`. Raw files go in `assets/incoming/rill/` and `assets/incoming/rill/clips/`. Required still keys are `hero-ridge`, `land-aerial`, `swale-close`, `root-zone-macro`, `systems-swale`, `systems-shade`, `systems-drip`, `field-note-01`, `field-note-02`, `field-note-03`, `season-ledger`, and `ecosystem-footer`. Required clips are `storm-threshold` and `root-zone-pulse`. Until files arrive, render labelled placeholders with fixed aspect ratios.\n\n## Verification\n\nAdd unit tests for all pure maths, the external store and validation. Add `tests/e2e/rill.spec.ts` for the route, scroll phase, 42 mm threshold, range keyboard behavior, form errors/success, placeholders, ecosystem footer, phone flow and reduced motion. Register `rill` in all six gallery registration points, run `npm run check`, `npm test`, `npx tsc --noEmit`, and `npx playwright test`.\n\n";

export const HIYAS_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"HIYAS\" (high-altitude expedition route planner)\n\nBuild a landing page in **Next.js (App Router) + React + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS v4** for a fictional mountaineering company called **HIYAS**. It plans and guides expeditions for climbers attempting one of the fourteen 8,000 m peaks, with route intelligence, Sherpa partnership, and a clear return plan.\n\nThe central metaphor is **an expedition is a chain of camps, so the page is a live route planner**. Choose a peak, change acclimatization days, choose a support mode, and redraw the route profile. Camp markers settle into the line and the locked plan carries into the local inquiry form.\n\n## Brand\n\n- Slogan: **Plan the line. Earn the summit.**\n- Positioning: **Guided eight-thousand-metre expeditions, planned with precision.**\n- Voice: clear, quiet, serious, human, prepared. Avoid reckless, heroic, guaranteed, tourist, or loud language.\n- Palette: black `#05070A`, dark blue `#0B2A4A`, white `#FFFFFF`. Do not add other hex colours; compose opacity and tints with `color-mix()`.\n- Type through `next/font/google`: Oswald 500/600/700 for display, Outfit 400/500/600 for body, Roboto Mono 400/500 for route data.\n- Geometry: sharp zero-radius panels, rules and controls. No rounded dashboard cards.\n- Footer: full-bleed mountain scene, sparse link columns and a giant HIYAS wordmark anchored into the image.\n\n## Sections\n\n1. Header: HIYAS mark, Plan, The fourteen, Support, Inquiry, selected route readout and one action.\n2. Hero: split editorial field with documentary expedition image, “Plan the line. Earn the summit.” and the first route line.\n3. Planner: peak selector for all fourteen mountains, SVG route profile, camp markers, 14–70 day range input, support mode select, and lock-plan action.\n4. The fourteen: dense index with real peak elevations, regions, route character and links back to the planner.\n5. Expedition architecture: Prepare, Approach, Acclimatize, Summit window, Return.\n6. Sherpa partnership: local leadership, route discipline and planned return, with image-led media slot.\n7. Logistics board: permits, weather, communications, oxygen, base systems and contingency.\n8. Field notes: route reports with documentary image placeholders.\n9. Inquiry: name, email, peak, days, support and note. Validate locally; success explicitly says no network request was sent.\n10. Footer: mountain image slot, columns, giant wordmark, coordinates and return-plan line.\n\n## Planner logic\n\nWrite tests first for `planner/logic.ts` and `forms/validation.ts`.\n\n- Peaks: Everest 8849 m, K2 8611 m, Kangchenjunga 8586 m, Lhotse 8516 m, Makalu 8485 m, Cho Oyu 8188 m, Dhaulagiri 8167 m, Manaslu 8163 m, Nanga Parbat 8126 m, Annapurna I 8091 m, Gasherbrum I 8080 m, Broad Peak 8051 m, Gasherbrum II 8035 m, Shishapangma 8027 m.\n- Days are visitor-selected planning inputs clamped to 14–70; the default is 21.\n- Support modes are Route only, Sherpa guided, and Private expedition.\n- Route segments are approach, acclimatize, summit, return.\n- Camp stops are Base camp, Camp 1, Camp 2, and Summit; the SVG profile is deterministic and capped at the selected elevation.\n\n## Motion and accessibility\n\nMotion identity is **technical / cinematic, showpiece**, with the verbs **plot** and **lock**. The signature moment is the route line drawing from base camp to summit while camp markers snap into place and the support plan resolves.\n\nUse Oswald display masks, SVG route stroke offsets, directional readout transitions, sharp rule draws, and one bounded page progress loop. Animate only transform, opacity, clip-path and SVG stroke offsets. Under reduced motion, remove route scrubs, parallax, tilt and ambient loops while keeping all content and final values visible. With JavaScript disabled, show the peak index and complete inquiry form in normal flow. Every control must have visible focus, pressed, disabled, loading and success states.\n\n## Media\n\nUse `docs/image-prompts/hiyas.md`. User-generated stills and clips go in `assets/incoming/hiyas/` and `assets/incoming/hiyas/clips/`. Required still keys are `hero-route`, `planner-profile`, `peak-everest`, `peak-k2`, `support-rope`, `camp-night`, `field-note-01`, `field-note-02`, `field-note-03`, and `footer-mountain`. Required clips are `route-dawn` and `camp-wind`. Until assets arrive, render labelled placeholders with fixed aspect ratios.\n\n## Verification\n\nAdd unit tests for planner logic, motion maths and inquiry validation. Add `tests/e2e/hiyas.spec.ts` covering planner selection, day range, support mode, locking, inquiry validation/success, footer scene, phone layout, reduced motion and no-JS. Register `hiyas` at all six gallery registration points, run `npm run check`, `npm test`, `npx tsc --noEmit`, and `npx playwright test`.\n\n";

export const KAZEKO_PROMPT = "# Prompt: rebuild \"Studio Kazeko\"\n\nBuild an isolated `/kazeko` landing page in Next.js 16 App Router, React 19, TypeScript and Tailwind CSS v4 for a fictional independent Japanese-inspired animation studio. Its motto is \"A little wonder. A world of feeling.\" The central idea: every film begins with a pencil mark, so scrolling turns an original countryside sketch into a coloured, moving scene.\n\n## Brand\nPalette: paper #F8F5E9, ink #243C35, meadow #64856A, leaf #C7D6A7, sky #D6E7EB, butter #EED998, clay #B96C51, muted ink #596C63. Use color-mix for tints. Display: Shippori Mincho 400, body: DM Sans 400/500, labels: IBM Plex Mono 400. Load via next/font/google; preload false for Japanese fonts. Original inline SVG bird-and-breeze mark, small STUDIO over the literary kazeko. wordmark. Mood: gentle, nostalgic, airy, painterly, curious. Soft 6px artwork corners, pill actions, editorial layouts, abundant negative space.\n\n## Sections\n1. Sticky cream header: logo, Our films / Our craft / The studio, and Let's make something. Phone menu is a native modal dialog with focus containment, Escape close and scroll locking.\n2. Full-width pale-blue sky hero: small independent-animation eyebrow, centred large serif two-line headline, \"We draw the little things. And the worlds they become.\", Step into our stories CTA. Bottom 72% is an original panoramic SVG landscape: layered rolling hills, little clay-roofed house, pale sun, paths, field hatching, wildflowers, clouds and paper bird. Paper silhouette overlaps the lower edge; three small captions finish the scene.\n3. Selected films in an asymmetric two-column grid: large portrait The Last Light, smaller wide Where the River Goes and A Pocketful of Spring. Each has an original illustrated placeholder, year, type, Japanese subtitle and accessible details dialog. Clearly describe these as fictional film concepts; do not pretend there is playable film footage.\n4. First, a line. Then, a little life. A 190vh sticky process scene has geometrically matching pencil and colour SVG layers. Progress = clamp((scrollY-start)/(sectionHeight-viewportHeight),0,1). Colour layer clip-path inset right = (1-progress)*100%. A labelled native range, Pencil / Colour / Life buttons and Resume scroll control support mouse/keyboard/touch. Explicit manual control persists until Resume. Phone and reduced-motion layouts unpin the stage.\n5. A small studio. A very open window. Portrait illustration of a countryside drawing desk and a thoughtful essay about noticing ordinary details; services are original shorts, brand animation, music videos and imagined worlds.\n6. Dark-green contact section: Something on your mind? Client-only form with name, email and idea. Validate trimmed required values, bounded lengths and sensible email format. Explain errors with aria-invalid and describedby. Focus first invalid field. Loading state becomes an accessible success panel, focused on arrival, with a folded bird and a downloadable text brief. Explicitly state nothing was sent or stored. Escape user text through React; never interpolate it into HTML.\n7. Cream footer with logo, motto, back-to-top link and fictional-studio disclosure. Global accessible pause/resume control for ambient movement.\n\n## Motion\nPersonality organic/analog; verbs drift and unfurl. One signature scene transforms pencil into colour. CSS-first load sequence: sky settles, header drops at 150ms, headline line masks at 250/350ms, body and CTA rise at 550/650ms. Use mask, rise, wipe, scale and drawn-line scroll reveals. Shared IntersectionObserver reveals once. Ambient clouds, grass and paper bird are at most three groups and pause offscreen, when hidden, and under user pause/reduced motion. Pure tested progress math; requestAnimationFrame throttled passive scroll listeners; no per-frame React state. Lenis is dynamically loaded only for fine desktop pointers without reduced motion. All observers/listeners/RAF/engines are cleaned up.\n\nTokens: strong ease-out cubic-bezier(.23,1,.32,1), expo cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1), handoff cubic-bezier(.77,0,.175,1), gentle spring; durations 90/180/420/800/1200ms; 24px shift, 70ms stagger. Hero stays visible without hydration. Reveal hiding only activates after JS setup; all content is visible without JS and under reduced motion. Gate hover transforms to fine pointers. Cards lift and illustrations zoom; buttons wipe and press to .97; fine-pointer CTAs have <=8px magnetism. Preserve visible keyboard rings and reserve all art boxes.\n\n## Assets, structure and verification\nKeep brand, sections, motion, process, forms and illustration modules inside the route group. page.tsx and content sections are server components; interactive leaves are client components. Include favicon, 1200×630 OG image, DESIGN.md, tested mechanic/validation, and e2e tests. Picture chooses generated 800/1600w AVIF/WebP via a manifest and otherwise shows labelled SVG concept art. Artwork is user-generated from docs/image-prompts/kazeko.md; raw assets go in assets/incoming/kazeko/. The hero and process scene remain original SVG for exact animation alignment. No API calls, stock assets or generation spend.\n\nRegister the palette, gallery metadata/prompt/preview, source-bundle dependencies (lenis only), image allowlist and shared e2e path. Test desktop and 390px touch, scroll controls, range keyboard operation, dialog focus and Escape, local form errors/success/download, navigation, reduced motion, no JS, overflow and page errors. Screenshot at 1440×900 and 390×844 plus 25/50/75% scroll. Check isolation, palette, unit tests, types and browser tests. Do not commit or deploy.\n";

